From 6eb1e051bf50a0dfa5a41eee6a0bca987bc27288 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 14:57:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 078/105] test: cover where a repeated pass resumes

The rule for placing a repeated pass is arithmetic over three numbers a
demuxer reports, and getting it wrong is not heard as a click at the seam: it
leaves a little of the item unplayed at every loop, and only after several of
them has enough drift gathered for the output's correction to saturate and
detune. That is a long way from the change that caused it, so pin it.

Move the decision into es_out_RepeatResume() and check it against the shapes
the demuxers actually report at the end of a 4 s item: matroska ending 48 ms
past a pcr it steps 23 ms at a time, ts and mpeg-ps whose ends sit inside a
step, wav whose end lands exactly on one, and the two edges - no end to take,
and an end behind the pcr.

The matroska case is the one that bites. With the end bounded by the cadence,
as it was until the previous commit, the test reports

    matroska: resumed at 3986001, expected 4011001

which is the 25 ms of its own content that every loop dropped.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
